## 3. Practice mapping — how the 450 questions are assigned

To make this maintainable, we'll use five questions per lesson as the initial allocation. That creates exactly 90 × 5 = 450 questions.

Each lesson gets five distinct challenge roles:

| Question slot | Type          | Purpose                                                      |
| ------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| A             | Foundation    | Apply the concept correctly                                  |
| B             | Business task | Solve a realistic dataset problem                            |
| C             | Edge case     | Handle nulls, ties, duplicates, schema issues, or boundaries |
| D             | Debugging     | Correct a flawed implementation                              |
| E             | Integration   | Combine the concept with earlier skills                      |

For example, lesson L026 (Left Semi and Left Anti Joins) would use D02 Banking:

| ID    | Question                                                                     |
| ----- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Q0126 | Find accounts with at least one posted transaction                           |
| Q0127 | Identify accounts without any transaction activity                           |
| Q0128 | Find transactions referencing nonexistent accounts, including null-key cases |
| Q0129 | Fix an incorrect anti-join that loses unmatched records                      |
| Q0130 | Reconcile unmatched transfers using anti joins and aggregations              |

That is much more useful than writing five nearly identical `join()` exercises.

We should preserve the category difficulty targets from v2 as guidance, but the five-per-lesson allocation is the exact ID scheme for this version. The individual difficulty assignments can be rebalanced during question authoring.

